{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} explorer; tree; disk drives; folders}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Explorer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This displays all drives and folders in a branching structure similar to File Manager.  Use it to give a broad view of your file system while you navigate through the folders.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc explorer.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Win95 Explorer style view
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 To combine t
he tree with an icon window for an interface like the Windows 95 Explorer, right click on the tree and check the "Show file window" menu item.  Now when you click on a node of the tree, the icon window is updated to display the contents of that folder.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Returning to the previously visited folder
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The File System Properties dialog lets you control whether the Explorer travels to the most recently visited folder the next time you open it.  It will never return to a folder from a removeable drive, in case th
e disk is not present.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b \line }{\uldb Icon windows}{\v IconWindows}
\par {\uldb Desktop Properties}{\v DesktopProp}
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Bin}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Wastepaper Bin}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} bin; trash; emptying the bin; restoring files; bin directories; locations; drives; [Bin Locations]; hiding the bin}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Wastepaper Bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par When you drop files or folders into the bin icon, they are moved to hidden directories instead of being deleted.  If you 
change your mind, you can restore them to their original location.  Otherwise, you'll need to empty the bin to free up the space used by the files.
\par 
\par Double click on the icon to open the bin window.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc bin.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How to restore files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 After selecting the items, choose Restore from the popup menu to move each file or folder back to where it came from.  Alternatively, drag the items to an icon window or shortcut.  This moves them all to that location.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Viewing folders in the bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can double click
 on a folder in the bin to show its contents in an icon window.  The binned files are placed in a temporary folder which may have tilde (~) characters and numbers in the name.  In general, you should not modify files and folders that are still in the bin,
 because Calmira may not be able to restore or keep track of them.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How to empty the bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The popup menu has Delete and Empty commands, which deletes selected items and all trash, respectively.  If you choose {\uldb Properties}{\v BinProp} from the menu, you can configure the bin to clear trash automatically.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using more than one bin directory
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
You can specify which drives to store trash on, and which folders to use.  If you have more than one hard disk drive, this could speed things up because trash from each drive can be moved quickly and need not be copied to another disk.
\par 
\par In the [Bin Locations] section, add mappings of drive=folder.  For example:
\par 
\par {\f5\fs18 [Bin locations]}{\f5\fs18 
\par }{\f5\fs18 c=c:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 windows}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 temp}{\f5\fs18 
\par }{\f5\fs18 d=d:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 waste}{\f5\fs18 
\par }{\f5\fs18 f=e:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 trash}{\f5\fs18 
\par }
\par This tells Calmira to store all files from drive F: in the folder e:\\Trash.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Automatically binning deleted files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can redirect all files deleted from Calmira's icon windows into the bin by selecting the "Move objects into bin instead of deleting" option from the {\uldb File System Properties dialog}{\v FileSystemProp}
.  When you press Del from an icon window, the effect is the same as dropping the objects into the bin icon.
\par When you choose this option and want to properly delete a file, press Shift+Del instead, or hold down shift when choosing Delete from the popup menu.
\par 
\par This setting does not affect files deleted by other programs, or from the Find Files dialog or Explorer tree.  You must manually clear out the bin to free up disk space, but remember that files could be stored on different drives so you need to check the 
[Bin locations] section if you need more space on a particular disk.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How to hide the bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Set its caption to an empty string, and restart Calmira.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Taskbar}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Taskbar}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} taskbar; applet tray; excluding programs; clock; time; programs; drag and drop; aliases; system menu; moving the taskbar; resizing the taskbar}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Taskbar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Each running process has a button on the bar and you can click on a button to switch to that task.  If you right click instead, a popup menu lets you minimize, maximize or close the window.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc taskbar.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par The start button has a special popup menu that lets you setup the Taskbar.  Choose {\uldb Taskbar Properties}{\v TaskbarProp} from this menu to
 adjust the settings.  You can configure the Taskbar to always stay on screen or hide when the cursor moves off it.  It will always pop up when the cursor reaches the bottom of the screen.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Drag and drop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If a program normally accepts files dropped into its icon from File Manager, you can drop files from Calmira into that program's taskbar button.  You can also copy and move files and folders by dropping them into the taskbar buttons for icon windows.  A h
idden taskbar will pop up when you drag objects to the base of the screen .
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The Applet Tray
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This is located on the Taskbar's clock, and it can hold a few icons for commonly used utilities.  These programs are treated slightly differently from those laun
ched by normal shortcuts.  Firstly, Calmira doesn't run a new copy of the program if it is already running.  Secondly, the applets are not shown on the Taskbar, since they can be brought forward from the Applet Tray.  You can add and remove applets from t
he Taskbar Properties dialog.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using aliases in the Applet Tray
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you need applet tray icons with more detailed settings (e.g. a specific icon), create a new subdirectory called "Tray" in Calmira's home directory and create some aliases there.  Open the Taskbar Properties dialog, and close it again to force the Apple
t Tray to reload.  The aliases will appear as small icons.
\par 
\par You cannot directly edit the icons that are in the tray -- you need to edit the original aliases and then reload them.  They behave like normal aliases, so each click will start a fresh instance of the program.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Excluding programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
There are some programs which you may not wish to appear Taskbar.  For example, programs which have icons that change appearance, like the Windows Clock, should stay as an icon on the desktop. Also, space on the Taskbar is valuable, so you should not show
 a Taskbar button for programs you don't often switch to.  Use the Exclusion tab page in the Taskbar Properties dialog to specify which programs to exclude.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Moving and resizing the taskbar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you drag the taskbar's
 body panel, you can move it to the top or bottom of the screen.  If you move the cursor to the inner edge of the taskbar, it changes to a double headed arrow.  This lets you resize the bar to show multiple rows of buttons.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Showing the system menu over buttons
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When you right click on a taskbar button, Calmira normally shows a popup menu with four standard commands.  If your programs have extra commands on their system menus, you can use the Taskbar Properties dialog to make the real system menu appear w
hen you right click.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} StartMenu}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Start menu}}
 {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} start menu; menu; properties}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This nested menu is displayed when you press Start on the Taskbar. Each menu item is equivalent to a program item in Program Manager.  Additionally, it can open new windows and dialogs, and programs that are launched from the Start Menu will be shown by y
our chosen icon on the Taskbar, even Windows programs.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc start.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par To edit the Start Menu, right click on the Start button and choose {\uldb Start Properties}{\v StartMenuProp} from the popup menu.  From there, you can also copy your program groups and icons from Program Manager.
\par 
\par The {\uldb System Properties dialog}{\v SystemProp} lets you choose whether to open the Start Menu when you right click over the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} icon.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} References}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Shortcuts and aliases}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} shortcuts; aliases; references; icons; desktop; properties; als; programs; files; folders; restoring shortcuts; arranging shortcuts}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Shortcuts and Aliases
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Shortcuts and aliases are {\i references}
 -- they point to another object such as a disk drive, folder, or file.  When you open or drop things into a reference, the effect is the same as if you had opened or dropped into the original object.  You can also create a link to an {\uldb Internet}{\v 
Internet} address.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Shortcuts
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 These are icons on the desktop, and you can create them in two ways: either drag an icon out of a window and release the mouse, or right click on the {\uldb desktop}{\v Desktop}
 and choose an item from the New Shortcut submenu.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc shortcut.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par The shortcut is saved in Calmira's INI file when you quit.  To edit its properties, click on its caption and choose {\uldb Properties}{\v ReferenceProp}
 from the menu.  Shortcuts are effective when used sparingly -- you would probably have shortcuts of your disk drives and a few important folders and utilities on your desktop.  Each shortcut is a real window and uses the same amount of resources.
\par 
\par Since shortcuts are ordinary desktop icons, some programs like the Windows task manager may re-arrange them to the bottom of the screen.  If this happens, you can use the Restore Shortcuts command from the desktop menu to move the back to their previous l
ocations.  Calmira has a replacement task manager that does not have such a problem (see {\uldb Installation}{\v Installing} for details).
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Aliases
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
These are small files (with an extension ALS) containing an icon and some data that points to the original object. To create aliases of visible objects, drag them to another folder or window, and hold down the Shift key before releasing the mouse.  To cre
ate an alias of something not currently visible, right click between the icons and choose Create Alias from the window menu. 
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc alias.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Aliases are just like other files, and you can copy, move or delete them without affecting the original object.  To change their properties, right click and select the Edit Alias command.
\par 
\par You can use aliases to give the effect of a free floating program group: move aliases into the one folder, create a shortcut of the folder, and change its icon.  They are also useful for creating "virtual links" to other folders, to change the apparent st
ructure of your directory tree.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Desktop}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Desktop}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 desktop; menu; icons; arranging icons; shortcuts; cascading windows; minimizing programs; repaint; properties; activating Calmira; opening folders; creating folders;}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }Desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par If you right click on your wallpaper, the desktop menu appears, which gives you fast access to frequently used features.  There are facilities to let you create a shortcut at the point where you clicked, and arrange desktop icons and windows.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Exposing hidden shortcuts
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When the desktop is hidden behind other windows, you can bring your shortcut icons forward by right clicking on the desktop while holding down the left mouse button.  If the desktop is covered by a maximized window, you use the same method on the Taskbar'
s main body panel, or just double click on the Taskbar.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Opening and creating folders
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The Open Folder command is particularly useful because it lets you quickly get to any directory without travelling through the tree.  To see only certain files, add a w
ildcard (which must include ? or *) after the folder name.  For example c:\\Windows\\System\\*.dll.
\par 
\par To create a new folder, select this menu command, type in a new folder name, and you will be asked whether it should be created.  The new folder is opened for you in a blank window.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Differences between Open Folder and Run dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can open an icon window from the {\uldb Run Program dialog}{\v RunProgram} by typing in a folder name, which gives you the benefit of a history list.  However, the Open Folder command is a
 little more powerful because it can create new folders, and you don't need to type in the full pathname -- if you omit a drive, Calmira uses the drive on which Windows is installed; if you omit a leading backslash, Calmira adds it for you.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 Some examples of what you can type for Open Folder:}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 Fully qualified folder name:}{\f2 \tab }{\f2 c:}{\f2 \\}{\f2 programs ---> c:}{\f2 \\}{\f2 programs}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 Leading backslash:}{\f2 \tab }{\f2 \tab }{\f2 \\}{\f2 programs   ---> c:}{\f2 \\}{\f2 programs}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 No leading punctuation:}{\f2 \tab }{\f2 \tab }{\f2 programs    ---> c:}{\f2 \\}{\f2 programs}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 Drive identifier:}{\f2 \tab }{\f2 \tab }{\f2 \tab }{\f2 a: --> a:}{\f2 \\}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When Calmira needs to fill in the full path, it uses the drive on which Windows is installed.  This is because the "current directory" has little meaning since Calmira operates across all drives.  This is different from the Open batch file -- if you type 
"Open programs" at the DOS prompt, Calmira assumes that you want to open the "programs" directory in the current directory (which now has a meaning).
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating a new folder on the desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Use the desktop menu to create a folder shortcut.  In the {\uldb Shortc}{\uldb ut Properties dialog}{\v ReferenceProp}
, enter a caption and the name of a new directory, then press OK.  Calmira will ask you whether you wish to create a new directory.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} TaskManager}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Task Manager}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} task manager; tasks; terminate; tile; cascade; arrange icons}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {
\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Task Manager
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc taskman.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Calmira is supplied with a replacement task manager program, CTaskman, which you can use as the default task manager by following the instructions in {\uldb Installation}{\v Installing}
.  You must be running Calmira for this new manager to be used, otherwise the Microsoft one is invoked.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Arranging windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Use the Ctrl and Shift keys together with the mouse to select tasks.  If you select more than one task, then the Tile, Cascade and Minimize commands will operate on your chosen tasks, otherwise they work on all tasks.

\par 
\par When the task manager first appears, no tasks are selected, so the arrangement features work on all tasks.  You can select everything in the listbox by pressing Ctrl+A.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Terminating a task
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The Terminate button ends a task abruptly, and you may lose resources or leave the system in an unstable state.  You should only use this command if the End Task button has no effect.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} FileManagement}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 File Management}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} file management; files; folders; copying; moving; dragging; drag and drop; deleting; renaming; refreshing windows; cut; copy; paste}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} File Management
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Copying
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Ensure the destination is visible, as a window or an icon.
\par 2.  Select which objects to copy.
\par 3.  Drag them to the destination and release the mouse.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par {\cf9 Alternative method:}{\cf9 
\par }1.  Select the objects to copy.
\par 2.  Press Ctrl+C.
\par 3.  Open the destination folder in an icon window.
\par 4.  Press Ctrl+V.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Moving
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Ensure the destination is visible, as a window or an icon.
\par 2.  Select which objects to move.
\par 3.  Drag them to the destination.
\par 4.  Right click {\i once}.
\par 5.  Release the mouse.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par {\cf9 Alternative method:}{\cf9 
\par }1.  Select the objects to move.
\par 2.  Press Ctrl+X.
\par 3.  Open the destination folder in an icon window.
\par 4.  Press Ctrl+V.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Deleting
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Select which objects to delete.
\par 2.  Right click over one of the selected icons.
\par 3.  Choose Delete from the popup menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Renaming
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Select an object to rename.
\par 2.  Right click over the icon.
\par 3.  Choose Rename from the popup menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating a folder
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Open the parent folder as an icon window.
\par 2.  Right click in the space between the icons.
\par 3.  Select Create Folder from the menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Refreshing windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you have used another program to change files or folders, existing icon windows must be refreshed to show the correct contents.  Press F5 or click on the left label on the status bar to read the directory again.

\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Working with descriptions}{\v Descriptions}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Descriptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Working with descriptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} descriptions; 4DOS; captions; filenames; descript.ion; merging directories}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Working with descriptions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par In the absence of long filenames in Windows 3.1, Calmira allows you to use 4DOS compatible  descriptions to label your files and folders.  When you upgrade to Windows 95 or Windows NT, you can turn the descriptions into proper long filenames.
\par 
\par Descriptions can either show up as popup hints (and an extra column in the list view), or be used as captions for the icons.  The {\uldb File System Properties}{\v FileSystemProp} dialog lets you adjust the settings.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding and changing descriptions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 After enabling the descri
ptions feature, open an icon window and select the files you want to describe.  Right click over one of them and choose the Describe command.  Calmira will prompt you for a description for each file.  Press Escape or the Cancel button to stop adding descr
iptions and save the descriptions you have accepted.
\par 
\par To delete descriptions, simply describe the files with empty strings.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Running 4DOS sessions simultaneously
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Calmira keeps a copy of the descriptions in memory for each icon window, but if you use a 4DOS box to manage files, it may modify the descriptions so that the Calmira's memory copy is out of date.  When you next use Calmira to copy files, the old descript
ions are carried across.
\par 
\par To prevent this, select the option to maintain up to date descriptions.  This causes descriptions to be read from disk {\i before} each update, to refresh the copy in memory.  There is still a risk in multitasking environments that co
nflicting updates will occur, so you are advised not to use two programs to modify the same directory at once.  Closing all icon windows will make it safe to modify the directory from the 4DOS prompt.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How descriptions are stored
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When a directory contains a file or folder that has a description, a hidden file called Descript.ion is created which stores the extra data.  When files are copied, moved or deleted, the description file is updated.  When folders are copied or moved, desc
ription files are handled automatically without asking for confirmation, even though they have the hidden attribute.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b\cf6 Warning}: Calmira 2.2 doesn't handle descriptions properly when you {\i merge} whole directory structures together \endash 
 the description files are merged on the assumption that all files are replaced, regardless of whether the file operations actually succeeded.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} KeyCommands}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Keyboard commands}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} keyboard; shortcuts; switching windows; hot-keys; browsing; refresh; cascade; arranging icons; parent folder; clos
e all; close others; close lower; close path; root; drives; undelete; find; explorer; printing; clipboard; copying filenames; sorting; finding files; cut; copy; paste; directory listings; closing windows}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Keyboard commands
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Most key combinations are shown in popup menus.  The following are available from icon windows, but are not shown in the menus:
