\pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Switching between windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Press Ctrl + Tab to cycle through each window in Calmira.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Browsing folders in one or more windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The {\uldb Desktop properties dialog}{\v DesktopProp} contains the setting {\i Browse through folders in the same icon window}.  If you hold down A
LT when double clicking on a folder icon, the folder is opened using the opposite of the current setting.
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Defining keyboard shortcuts}{\v KeyboardShortcuts}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} SelectingObjects}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Selecting objects}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} selecting; deselecting; highlighting; wildcards; lasso; block select; range select}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Selecting objects
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 When you click on an icon, it becomes focused (with a dotted box around it).  It is also selected if it has a grey background.  Some file operations wo}{\f2 rk on all selected items, others just work on the focused one.}
{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To select a single item
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 Click on the icon with the left mouse button.  }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To select a range
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 1.  Select the first item in the range.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 2.  Hold down SHIFT.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 3.  Select the last item in the range. }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To toggle the selection of a single item
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 Hold down CTRL when you left-click on it.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To select a block
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 Press the left mouse button when the cursor is to the left of an icon.  Then stretch the lasso around the icons you wish to select.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard \qc {\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc lasso.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2 
\par }\pard {\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To de-select items
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 Click on an unselected object or click on an empty space.  }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To select or de-select everything in the window
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Left click on the right hand label (shift-click if there is only one label displayed).  If there are items currently highlighted, all items are de-selected.  Otherwise everything is selected.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 To select files using wildcards
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Right click on the right hand label to open the Select Files box.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Internet}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Internet}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} Internet;URLs;web pages;shortcuts
; aliases; run program dialog; start menu; web browsers; browers; serviceapplication; Netscape Navigator; Internet Explorer; prefixes; [Internet]; bookmarks; DDE; favourites; http://}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Internet features
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Opening URLs through DDE
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira is set up to use Microsoft Internet Explorer 3.0 installed in c:\\IExplore.  To specify its directory, or use  another browser which supports DDE (Dynamic Data Exchange), follow these steps:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1. Open the {\uldb System Properties dialog}{\v SystemProp}.
\par 2. Select the Advanced tab page.
\par 3. Open the [Internet] section.
\par 4. Modify the ServiceApplication string to point to your browser.
\par     {\i Do} include the full pathname.
\par     {\i Do not} include the .EXE file extension.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par For example, 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [Internet]
\par ServiceApplication=c:\\netscape\\netscape 
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par would be suitable for Netscape Navigator 3.0.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Opening URLs using a command line
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some browsers may not work correctly using DDE, especially if they take a long time to load, causing a DDE timeout.  In these cases, Calmir
a can open an Internet shortcut by running a specific program.  If the CommandLine string in the [Internet] section is present, it will override any DDE settings.  Use the %url% variable to insert the address.  
\par 
\par To open URLs using Navigator 4.0, you can add:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [Internet]
\par CommandLine=navstart.exe -browser %url%
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 For Internet Explorer 3.0, you can try:
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 
\par [Internet]
\par CommandLine=iexplore.exe %url%
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Specifying URL prefixes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
In the [Internet] section, the URLPrefixes setting determines the strings that Calmira uses to identify an Internet address.  When it processes a command line, Calmira looks for the prefixes at the beginning of the command, and calls your web browser if o
ne is found.  
\par 
\par You can modify this setting to include any additional prefixes such as "www." or "ftp.".  Most web browsers now allow you to omit the protocol indicator.{\b 
\par }{\b 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Shortcuts and aliases
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Use these to quickly mark a web page or other Internet address.  When you create a new {\uldb shortcut or alias}{\v References} and choose {\i Internet}
 as the type, Calmira looks for your browser program.  If the browser is running, the current location's title and URL are captured for you.  Otherwise, you must enter the details yourself.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Run dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can type a URL into the {\uldb Run Program dialog}{\v RunProgram} to open it.  Your browser is launched if it is not already running.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Type in a URL into the {\i Command line}
 field, and the effect is the same as for the Run Dialog.  You can use the Start Menu to organise Navigator style bookmarks missing from the Internet Explorer 3.0 Favourites feature.
\par 
\par To avoid having to keep the {\uldb Start Menu Properties dialog}{\v StartMenuProp}
 open while you are browsing (or paste URLs manually), create aliases of URLs in a temporary folder.  Afterwards, the aliases can be dropped into the dialog's menu tree, which automatically converts the captions and addresses.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} BinProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Bin Properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} bin; trash; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {
\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Bin Properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart DialogBoxes}{\*\bkmkend DialogBoxes}
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc binprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This controls the settings of the {\uldb wastepaper bin}{\v Bin}.  You can access it by opening the bin window, and choosing Properties from the popup menu.  When the bin is hidden, the dialog can be opened from the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} window.

\par 
\par Calmira does not automatically bin items from removeable media such as floppy disks. If you choose to use the bin for the Delete command, 
objects from floppy disks are deleted immediately.  To bin items from floppy disks, you need to drop them into the bin icon.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} DesktopProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Desktop Properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} desktop; minimize; closing windows; cursor; flicker; browsing; tasks; windows; options; settings; properties; dialog boxes}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Desktop Properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc deskprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog contains settings that are associated with window management as well as those relating to the desktop.  To open it, right click on the wallpaper and choose Properties from the popup menu.  If you've disabled the desktop popup menu, you can ope
n this dialog from the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} window.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Right click on minimize/maximize box to close
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Enable this setting to let you close any icon window by right clicking on the minimize/maximize buttons (as a substitute for Win95's new close button).  You need to run the {\uldb Taskbar}{\v Taskbar}
 for this to work, because it relies on the Taskbar's mouse controller.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Close the window when right button is released
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
This setting works in conjunction with the previous one.  Turn it off to allow windows to be closed quickly without activating them or bringing them to the front.  Turn it on to prevent some programs from displaying a popup menu immediately after you clos
e a window.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Animate cursor when dragging files over desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Turn off this option if your cursor flickers or disappears completely when dragging over the desktop.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Browse through folders in the same icon window
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you hold down the ALT key while opening a folder, this setting is temporarily reversed.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Treat Calmira windows as separate task windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Turn this on to prevent all Calmira windows and shortcuts from being brought forward whenever you click on one of them.  However, this feature also causes the windows to appear in Task Manager and in the Alt+Tab list 
because they now belong to the desktop.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} FileProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 File and Folder properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} files; folders; properties; attributes; associating files; registry; version information; dialog boxes}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} File and Folder properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc fileprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Open this dialog from {\uldb icon windows}{\v IconWindows} by selecting some objects, and choosing Properties from the objects' popup menu.  You will receive different information depending on whether you selected a file, a folder or multiple items.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 File attributes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you select multiple items, some check boxes may be greyed, which indicates that items differ in this attribute.  Leave a check box greyed to prevent the particular attribute from being modified.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Associating programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When type in your own command to run, you need to include %1 on the command line where the document filename should be inserted.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Version information
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Under Windows 3.1, only version information compiled by a Microsoft resource compiler can be reliably detected.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} FileSystemProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 File System properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} file system; files; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes; icons; captions; managing files}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} File System properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc fsysprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog controls the built-in file manager.  To open it, choose File System from an {\uldb icon window's}{\v IconWindows} popup menu, or open it via the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} window. For details about file descriptions, see {\uldb 
Working with descriptions}{\v Descriptions}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Show miniature versions of icons
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Turn this setting on to shrink large icons down to 16 x 16 when displaying small icons or list view (it only affects icon windows, not the {\uldb Bin}{\v Bin} or {\uldb Find Files dialog}{\v FindFiles}
).  This display mode is slow!
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Show folder/drive names for icon window captions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When this is turned on, icon windows display just the 8 letter directory name on icon windows, and root windows show the drive's caption from the Computer.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Do not show file extension for files with own icon
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira compares each icon to the blank fi
le icon before writing the caption.  If this option is on, and the icon is not the blank file icon, then the 3 letter extension is truncated.  This feature is useful for reducing clutter, but if more than one file type uses the same icon (e.g. both GIF an
d JPG types use a graphics icon), then you won't be able to distinguish between files with the same 8 letter name.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Move objects into bin instead of deleting
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 See the {\uldb Wastepaper Bin}{\v Bin} topic for details about using this switch.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} FindFiles}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Find Files dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} find files dialog; finding files; searching for files; folders; open with dialog; speed; wildcards; dialog boxes}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Find Files dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc find.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog lets you rapidly search for files in the background.  You can open it from the {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}, desktop popup menu, {\uldb Computer}{\v computer} window or {\uldb icon windows}{\v IconWindows}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Searching for files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
To look for multiple sets of files, separate each wildcard with a semi-colon.  When you press the Search button, the search takes place in the background and you can carry on with other tasks.  The cursor changes to a pointer with an hourglass attached, a
nd returns to normal when the search is complete.  Files and folders are displayed in the listbox as soon as they are found.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Searching for substrings
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you search for a file string that does not contain a dot, Calmira will return all the filenames cont
aining that string at any position.  For example, if you search for "win", you might receive win.com, windows, win.ini and config.win.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Opening an item
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Double click on an item to open it, or right click on the listbox to display commands that let you open an item's parent folder or delete it.  Ctrl and Shift can be used to select multiple items, and they can be dropped into other programs.  To open a fil
e using a specific program, right click and choose {\uldb Open With}{\v OpenWith}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The search speed
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira tries to ensure that there are no duplicates in the listbox.  You can gain maximum search speed if you specify only one wildcard for each search {\i and} clear the listbox between searches.
\par 
\par {\b Tip}: to search an entire drive, just enter a single drive letter in the {\i Start From} box.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} RunProgram}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Run Program dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} run program dialog; running programs; launching programs; programs; folders; opening folders; documents; DOS commands; dialog boxes; MS-DOS}}
 {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Run Program dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc run.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog lets you start a Windows or MS-DOS program, or execute an MS-DOS command.  You can open it from the {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}, desktop menu, {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} window or {\uldb icon windows}{\v IconWindows}.
\par 
\par If you check the DOS command{\b  }check box, the text you type in is passed to your command interpreter so you can obtain directory listings etc..  Otherwise, the text is passed to Windows to execute.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} OpenWith}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Open With dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} open file with dialog; dialog boxes; documents; files; programs; opening files; [File Viewers]; %filename%; parameters; command line}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Open With dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc openwith.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog lets you open a file with a particular program.  Select a file in an icon window, right click over it and choose Open With from the popup menu.  Type the name of a program into the edit box and press OK.
\par 
\par You can also choose a program from the listbox by double clicking on an item.  If you single click instead, the program's details appear in the edit box and you can edit the command before pressing OK.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding programs to the dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The [File Viewers] section of the INI file lets you add entries to the listbox.  Each line should be in the format <caption>=<command>. For example:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [File Viewers]
\par Notepad=c:\\windows\\notepad.exe
\par Write=c:\\windows\\write.exe
\par Pif Editor=c:\\windows\\pifedit.exe
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par You can include parameters within the command lines, and if you need to place the name of the document file at a specific point, insert the environment string %filename% at the required position.  For example, 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 viewer %filename% /p
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} StartMenuProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Start Menu properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 start menu; menus; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes; bitmaps; icons; creating menu items; program manager; programs; documents; folders; sub-menus; copying; moving; commands; editing menu items; separators; columns; breaks; menu breaks; keyboa
rd; accelerators; ampersand; hot-keys; computer; rebuilding start menu}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Start Menu properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc strtprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Open this dialog by right clicking on the Start button of the {\uldb Taskbar}, and choose Start Properties.  This dialog is not modal, so you can switch windows and drop items into the tree.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating new items
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 There are three ways of doing this:
\par 
\par 1.  Press the Add or Insert buttons on the toolbar.
\par 2.  Drag and drop files and folders from icon windows straight into the tree.
\par 3.  Press the Import Groups button to copy items from Program Manager.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating new sub-menus
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 A sub-menu starts off as a normal menu item.  When you use the Insert button or drag nodes onto it, it becomes a menu.  When the contents are deleted, it becomes an item again.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Copying and moving items and sub-menus
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Simply drag a node's caption to move it.  To drop a node into a closed sub-menu, hold down ALT before releasing the mouse button.  To copy a single item, hold down the CTRL key when dropping.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Editing an item
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can double click on a node without descendants to edit it.  Otherwise, click on the Edit button, press Enter, or right click for the popup menu.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Built-in menu commands
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Instead of specifying a program to run, the {\i command line} field of a menu item can open or activate a window in Calmira.  The recognised commands are in the drop-down list.  The $Folder command opens an {\uldb 
icon window}{\v IconWindows}, and requires a directory name as parameter.  If wildcard characters * or ? are present in the parameter, they are used as the filter.  For example:
\par 
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx3012\cellx7332\pard \s5\intbl {\f5\fs18 $Folder c:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 windows}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 *.exe}{\f5 \cell }displays all the executables\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \trowd 
\trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx3012\cellx7332\pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\f5\fs18 $F}{\f5\fs18 older c:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 windows}{\f5 \cell }displays all files\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5 
\f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The icon property
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 To use a Windows bitmap (bmp) file for a menu item instead of an icon, type in the full name of the bitmap file in the {\i Icon from} edit box. This bitmap should be 16 x 16 pixels, but can have any number of colours.  

\par 
\par To show a menu item without any icon (which also saves memory), specify <none> in the icon field.  If you just leave it blank, Calmira picks the icon for you.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding menu separators
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 A dash "-" as the first character of a caption will turn the entry into a separator line.  Each item must have a different caption, so use something like -1, -2 ... for multiple separators.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Breaking menus into columns
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can break a large menu into {\i equal sized} columns.  Edit the {\b parent menu} item's caption, and insert *<
number> at the front of the string.  For example to break the Games menu into three equal columns, change the caption to *3Games.  The *3 will not be shown in the actual menu, but you need to include it when assigning a {\uldb hot-key}{\v 
KeyboardShortcuts}.
\par 
\par If you want to break up a menu into columns which are not equal, you can create dummy menu items to fill out the space. The trick is to make use of the "*n" column dividers within the columns themselves!  This allows you to create unique items with blank 
captions -- you can use "*1", "*2"..."*9" and Calmira will chop off the asterisks and digits.  Finally, set the icon to <none>.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using the keyboard with the Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 An ampersand "&" in front of any letter in the caption will underline it for use as th
e accelerator.  To enable the keyboard for use with the Start Menu, Calmira must grab the focus whenever the menu appears.  Turn this feature on in the Preferences page.  You can also specify a {\uldb keyboard shortcut}{\v KeyboardShortcuts}
 ("hot-key") to bring up the Start Menu.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding items through the Computer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can drag and drop objects onto the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} icon, and they will appear in the Start Menu Properties dialog the next time you open it.  This saves you from having to keep the dialog on screen fo
r long periods.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Rebuilding the Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you are displaying a Windows 95 style menu with icons, the icons need to be gathered up before the Start Menu can be displayed again.  The "rebuild in background" checkbox lets you carry on with other tasks while this occurs -- the cursor changes to a 
pointer with an hourglass attached, and returns to normal when the Start Menu is ready for use.
\par 
\par If you add, move, delete items, or change icons, then a full rebuild is required.  If you only edit the caption, working folder or show modes of menu items, the menu will be automatically rebuilt without reading the icons again, which is much faster.  

\par 
\par Note: if you have modified icon or bitmap files used for the menu, you must either edit an icon
\par or command field, or click on the Rebuild checkbox (twice if it is already checked).  This forces a full rebuild.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding items from installation programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If Calmira is running as the shell, it can detect input from installation programs, and create new Start Menu ite
ms instead of Program Manager icons.  When an installation program has finished running, open the Start Menu Properties dialog, and the new groups and items will appear in the tree.  Press OK to accept the new entries, or Cancel to discard them.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Environment variables}{\v Environment}
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} SystemProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 System Properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} system; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes; colours; advanced settings; INI files; sounds; network connections}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} System Properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc sysprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog contains global, screen display and network settings.  Open it from the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} window by right clicking between the icons and choosing Properties at the top of the menu.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Play system sounds when displaying messages
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The sound files are the ones that you choose from Control Panel.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Display page colours
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some of the "colour" names are actually Windows screen elements.  Use these "colours" to match Calmira's colour scheme to your current Windows colour scheme.  For
 example, assign the BtnFace colour to something which should always stay the same colour as Windows buttons.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Network page
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The Network page is for local area networks, and allows you to connect directly to a device, bypassing the standard Windows for Workgroups dialog boxes. Type in a remote share name (usually in the form \\\\Server\\
Resource) and password, and the Computer window will be updated with new drives when you close the dialog. Ask your network administrator for a list of shared resources.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Advanced settings
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The Advanced page contains an INI file editor that lets you modify Calmira's configuration file without the need to quit Calmira or use a separate text editor.  Double click on a section name or click on a book icon to show the settings it contains.  The 
identifiers on the left are not case sensitive, but the values on the right {\i are}.  The order in which lines appear does not usually matter.
\par 
\par Many of the INI file settings can be adjusted through various dialog boxes, and these should be left alone.  You should also leave settings that are updated automatically, such as window positions.
\par 
\par {\b Note}: Some settings will not take effect until you restart the program.
\par 
\par {\b See also:}\line {\uldb Miscellaneous Settings}{\v MiscSettings}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} ReferenceProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Shortcut and Alias Properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 shortcuts; aliases; references; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes; changing shortcut type; parameters; use document folder; drag and drop}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}}
 Shortcut and Alias Properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc refprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par This dialog lets you modify any {\uldb reference}{\v References}.  To edit a shortcut, click once on its caption and choose Properties.  To edit an alias, right click on it and choose the Edit Alias command.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Changing the shortcut or alias type
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Move the cursor over the label of the second edit box, and the cursor shape changes to a pointing hand.  Click on the label to cycle through all the different types.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Parameters field
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
As well as typing in parameters, you can specify where in the command line to insert the names of files which are dropped into the shortcut or alias.  Place the string %droppedfiles% at the desired location, and when you drop any files, it will be replace
d with filenames.  
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Use document folder setting
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This controls how dropped files are passed to programs via the command line.  
\par 
\par When the box is checked, only the filenames are passed, and the location of the first file is temporarily used as the worki
ng folder.  Since the length of the command line is limited, this feature allows more files to be dropped, but might not work with all programs.
\par 
\par When the box is unchecked, the full pathnames are passed.
\par 
\par See also:
\par {\uldb Environment strings}{\v Environment}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} TaskbarProp}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Taskbar Properties dialog}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} taskbar; properties; options; settings; dialog boxes; refresh interval; excluding programs; spy; class names; programs; start button}}
 {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Taskbar Properties dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc taskprop.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Open this dialog by right clicking on the Start button and choosing Taskbar Properties.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Refresh interval setting
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This determines how often the Taskbar updates the button captions and removes any buttons for windows that have disappeared from the screen without being detected in the normal way.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding items to the Applet Tray
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you press the Add button, you are prompted for a new entry.  Enter the popup hint to be displayed, an equals sign, and the program's full pathname.  For example:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 Calculator=c:\\windows\\calc.exe
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par You can only add Windows programs to the applet tray, and these programs should only use one main window, otherwise they may not be restored or re-activated correctly.
\par 
\par See the {\uldb Taskbar}{\v Taskbar} topic for information about using aliases in the applet tray.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding exclusions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 To exclude an ordinary program such as the Windows clock, just add its full pathname to the list.  
\par 
\par If a program shows more than one window, the optional window class name lets you exclude only certain types of windows.  You can find class names by selecting {\i Spy}
 from the Start button's menu, and watching the button hints, which will now tell you the filename and class of each task.  When you have found the class name, separate it from the filename with a single space.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} CommonProbs}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Common Problems}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 troubleshooting; common problems; errors; problems; resources; video drivers; incompatibilities; Adobe Type Manager; icons; display; start menu; shell; program manager; wallpaper; palette; hot-keys; directories; programs; working folder; stream read erro
r; out of resources; disappearing icons; corrupted graphics; fonts; text alignment; crashes; start menu; access denied error; taskbar; web browsers; internet; URLs; keyboard}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {
\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Common Problems
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart Troubleshooting}{\*\bkmkend Troubleshooting}
\par This section contains a list of more frequent problems and their possible remedies.  You can also find useful information in the {\uldb Questions and Answers}{\v QandA} section.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 "Out of resources" error when Program Manager shows plenty
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 See the Video Driver Incompatibilities section below
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Icons disappear randomly and/or Start Menu is partially visible
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This is a known problem caused by Adobe Type Manager (ATM).  If you are using ATM 2.0 or 2.5, manually edit your System.ini file and comment out the following two lines by adding semi-colons in front of them:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 system.drv=atmsys.drv
\par atm.system.drv=system.drv
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Contact Adobe to receive the latest drivers.  If you are using ATM 3.0 or higher, add the setting ATMIcon=off to the [Settings] section of Atm.ini.  This should allow Calmira to be displayed correctly, but there will be no ATM icon at the bottom-left of t
he screen.
\par 
\par You should see the Video Driver Incompatibilities section below.  Sometimes, this problem does not occur if you run Calmira as an ordinary application, using Program Manager as the shell.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Calmira doesn't run as the Windows shell
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you get the error message "Cannot run progman" or a similar one, check that you've included the full pathname of Calmira in the shell= line.  
\par 
\par You can run Windows by typing WIN /B at the DOS prompt, which creates the Bootlog.txt file in the Windows directory.  Look at the last few entries in the file.  If Calmira fails to load due to "return code 3", try moving all the Calmira files into the Win
dows directory and editing the shell= line again.
\par 
\par If a driver or DLL has failed to load, Calmira may be incompatible with that file for some reason; there have been reports that replacing the file with a different version may solve the problem.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Program Manager loads even when Calmira is the shell
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can check whether Calmira really is the shell by opening the Shutdown dialog from the {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}
 -- the Quit Calmira option is disabled when this is the case.  If so, make sure Program Manager is not being loaded from elsewhere, such as the Load= and Run= lines of Win.ini, the Startup menu, or another utility.  If the Quit Calmira option {\i is}
 enabled, then your System.ini may contain another shell= line above the one specifying Calmira.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Wallpaper palette is not restored correctly
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
This is a known problem on 256 colour displays when Calmira is used with some programs that require an extended palette, i.e., more than just the 20 system colours reserved by Windows.  It also occurs with other programs when Calmira is not running.  The 
effect can be minimised if you use a 16 colour wallpaper, or you can use the Repaint Screen command from the desktop menu when required.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Keyboard shortcuts don't work
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Make sure you have specified the {\uldb key combinations}{\v KeyboardShortcuts}
 correctly -- you need to add two or more keys from the set (Ctrl, Alt, Shift).  If you only specify Ctrl+A, for example, Calmira ignores the setting.  Also check that the caption you entered still exactly matches the {\uldb shortcut}{\v References} or {
\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu} item's caption.  For menu items, remember to include the ampersands (&) and any menu-break specifiers like *2.
\par 
\par You may need to disable other utilities which monitor the keyboard for special key combinations, although Calmira should work with well-behaved utilities like QuicKeys from Microsoft.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Programs can't find files in their home directory
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some programs need to be started from their home directory so that they can find INI files and other special files.  If you receive "Cannot find file" errors, you should use their home directo
ry as the Working Folder field when creating shortcuts, aliases or Start Menu items.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 "Stream read error" when opening an icon window
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This could occur if Calmira tries to interpret a normal file as an {\uldb alias}{\v References}
.  By default, Calmira assumes that all files with an extension of ALS are alias files.  If there is a problem with this, you can move non-alias ALS files into a separate folder, or change the alias extension which Calmira uses (see {\uldb 
Miscellaneous Settings}{\v MiscSettings}).
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Text and labels are mis-aligned
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira was designed using small screen fonts, and some of the windows and dialog boxes may look untidy when used with large fonts.  You can try changing some of the fonts by editing the INI file (see {\uldb 
Miscellaneous Settings}{\v MiscSettings}), otherwise, there is nothing you can do to improve the display except run Windows setup and choose a video driver that uses small fonts.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 General Protection Fault in module VDOACM16.DLL
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira may not be compatible with this file, so if you encounter the above message, you need to disable the piece of software that is using it.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Programs crash when used with Calmira {\i or}
\par Calmira crashes when used with another program
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Serious problems should rarely occur, but if they do, the most likely culprit is the Taskbar, because it interacts with other programs in unusual ways, such as resizing and moving their windows.  
\par 
\par You can turn off these settings in the Taskbar Properties dialog to try and find the exact feature which is causing the problem:
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Keep Taskbar on screen all the time
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Fit maximised windows to space above Taskbar
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Move minimized window icons above the bar
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Hide the icons of minimized applications
\par 
\par To stop the Taskbar from intercepting mouse messages, add the line UseMouseHook=0 to the [Taskbar] section of Calmira.ini.  This may avoid some conflicts, but then the Taskbar won't be able to hide itself automatically.
\par 
\par Please read the {\uldb General Troubleshooting}{\v Trouble} section for more help.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Some Program Manager groups are not imported into the Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
On some computers, not all of the groups or items get imported in the Program Group Convert dialog.  This seems to be dependent on Program Manager or the DDE interfacing, since problematic group files can successfully be converted on another computer.  Th
ere is no known solution at present.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 "Access denied" message when copying or moving a file
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Check that the file has not been opened by another application which is still running, e.g. you need to close Microsoft Word documents before they can be copied.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Cursor flickers or disappears when dragging across the desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Turn off the Animate Cursor setting in the {\uldb Desktop Properties dialog}{\v DesktopProp}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 New programs don't appear immediately on the Taskbar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira doesn't show a button for a window until it has received an Activate message, which is sometimes lost or not sent.  Click on a different 
window, and click on the new application again to allow the Taskbar to detect the new program.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Small icons always appear faint
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This could be due to the different ways that video drivers shrink bitmaps.  You can try setting the darker stretch modes in the {\uldb Start Menu Properties}{\v StartMenuProp} and {\uldb Taskbar Properties}{\v TaskbarProp
} dialogs, but you may need to change your video driver to get better results.  See the screenshots in this help file for an example of roughly what the icons {\i should} look like.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Start Menu and Taskbar graphics suddenly change colour
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some programs which use an extended 256 colour palette can inadvertently modify the palette used by Calmira's bitmaps, replacing one colour with another.  You need to rebuild the {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}
 or Calmira itself to restore the bitmaps' palettes.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Video driver incompatibilities
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira may not work correctly with some video drivers that use more than 256 colours, or video drivers which are too old or badly written. Symptoms may include:
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} "Out of resources" errors when resources are not low
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Disappearing icons in various windows
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} A partially visible Start Menu
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} Start Menu icons becoming random pixels
\par 
\par You should install the standard 640x480 16 colour VGA driver from Microsoft to test if the problem lies in the video driver.  If so, contact your graphics card manufacturer for a driver update, or use generic 256 colour Super VGA drivers from Microsoft, w
hich are highly recommended.  They are often supplied on CD-ROMs from magazines, or you can download them from {\cf9 ftp.microsoft.com}.
\par 
\par High-end video cards may also cause some of these symptoms if they perform various graphics optimisations.  You can try turning off the optimisations from the video driver's configuration program.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 URLs are not opened by web browser
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
For Calmira's Internet features to work, you need a web browser that supports the standard DDE interface defined by Spyglass Inc.  This includes Netscape Navigator 2.0/3.0 and Microsoft Internet Explorer 3.0.  Netscape Communicator users, see the {\uldb 
Internet}{\v Internet} topic for help.
\par 
\par Sometimes repeating an action (e.g. clicking on a shortcut) should open the URL correctly.  You may also experience p
roblems if your browser takes too long to load, causing the DDE system to time-out.  This may simply have no effect, or you may receive error messages, in which case, you must launch the browser using the command line setting.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 WinZip says "Incompatible Program Manager replacement"
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some setup programs query Program Manager for information about current program groups and items, but due to a technology limitation, Calmira does not return information about the Start Menu to other programs.  
\par 
\par If you rece
ive an error message saying that there is an incompatible Program Manager replacement, you need to turn off the option that lets install programs modify the menu.  When you do this, Program Manager will be loaded every time you install something.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} KnownIssues}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Known Issues}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 known issues; problems; errors; icons; Alt+Tab; programs; icon windows; popup hints; highlight bar; start menu; taskbar; filters; keyboard; mouse; Win32s; explorer; bin; find files dialog; progress box; cursor; dragging; wi
ldcards; hints; drag and drop; DDE; MS-DOS; DOS programs}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Known issues
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 MS-DOS programs do not show the correct icon in Alt+Tab
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This feature requires a particular shell DDE service for MS-DOS programs which Calmira does not implement.  Graphics mode is used most of the time, and the {\uldb Taskbar}{\v Taskbar}
 shows your chosen icons for MS-DOS programs.  The Taskbar goes further than the shell DDE because it can show a different icon for each instance of a program, whether it is MS-DOS or Windows.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Install programs cannot retrieve Start Menu information
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When Calmira is the shell, installation programs can add items to the Start Menu but cannot obtain information about the submenus and menu items.  This is because Calmira does not respond to the DDE command that requests program group information, and a f
uture version may implement this feature.  If an installation program does not work correctly, turn off the install feature in the Start Menu Properties dialog.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Icon windows can show the same file or folder multiple times
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The wildcards used in file filters for {\uldb icon windows}{\v IconWindows}
 must be mutually exclusive.  For example, *.exe and *.com are mutually exclusive because a file cannot have both an EXE and COM extension.  If the filters overlap, you will end up with duplicate files, probably resulting in errors.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Taskbar icons are different when the Taskbar is reloaded
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some icons for the Taskbar are obtained directly from Calmira when programs are launched.  This includes programs that are started
 from shortcuts, aliases or the Start Menu, and the Taskbar does not retrieve the same icons if you unload the module and run it again.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Word for Windows 2.0 runs WinHelp if you click on the maximized caption
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This is a side effect of preventing maximized windows from being moved.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 New file associations do not change document icons
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you associate a file through the {\uldb File Properties dialog}{\v FileProp}, there will be no effect on the icons displayed until you restart Calmira.  However, the correct program
 will be launched when you double click on a document.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Extended ASCII filename characters not raised or lowered properly
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you create filenames using accented characters, these will not be displayed properly in Calmira because accented upper case characters are not converted to the correct lower case equivalents.  The underlying MS-DOS file system may or may not distinguis
h the filenames converted by Calmira; do not use Calmira to modify these files if you are in doubt.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Popup hints do not show descriptions in Small Icons view
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The hints only work as expected in the Large Icons view.  To show descriptions with small icons, you need to use the List view and enable the description column.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Shortcuts can be re-arranged by Task Manager and other programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 All of Calmira's icons on the desktop are implemented as ordinary minimized windows, so it is possible for programs such as the Task Manager to move the icons to the bottom of the screen as if they were applications.  

\par 
\par If this happens, you can use the Restore Shortcuts command on the desktop menu, which moves the shortcut icons back to their last saved position. Calmira's own icon arrangement commands accessed from the desktop menu will not disturb the placement of shor
tcuts.  Also, Calmira's own task manager will respect shortcut placement.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Pixels on Start Menu icons are turned blue by the highlight bar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu} uses bitmaps instead of icons.  Bitmaps use less memory because they don't have a transpare
ncy mask, and the drawback is that they can only use simulated transparency by replacing all pixels of one colour (silver in this case) with another colour (the blue from the highlight).
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The Taskbar is not hidden when the cursor moves over a scroll bar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The {\uldb Taskbar}{\v Taskbar} only detects mouse movements over the client area of windows, which does not include window frames or scroll bars.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Icon windows do not refresh automatically like File Manager
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira does not detect notification messages about changes in the file system.  You must refresh {\uldb icon windows}{\v IconWindows} manually when some files have changed.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Filters, column choices and icon view modes are not saved
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Icon windows are always opened with the settings from the {\uldb File System Properties dialog}{\v FileSystemProp}, even when their screen positions are saved.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Calmira's keyboard and mouse features do not work in Win32s programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira is a 16-bit Windows program, and the windows hooks, which it uses to detect messages being sent, are not triggered by 32-bit programs because they run in a slightly different environment.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Hot-key assignments are activated when using Alt Gr
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The Alt Gr key generates the same key combination as Ctrl+Alt.  Do not assign a command to Ctrl+Alt+<key> if you normally use Alt Gr+<key> to enter a special character.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Explorer tree's file pane closes with no error message
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you are exploring a hard disk and then click on the {\uldb Explorer tree}{\v Explorer} node of a floppy drive that doesn't contain a disk, the file pane closes automatically.  It is behaving like all other {\uldb 
icon windows}{\v IconWindows}, which close when their directory is no longer accessible.  If you click on the floppy drive again, you will receive an error message.  A similar problem can be observed with shortcuts.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Files cannot be binned from the Find Files dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The {\uldb Bin}{\v Bin} only accepts files and folders from icon windows.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Icons "drawn" by programs are not reflected in Taskbar buttons
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some programs paint their own minimised icons instead of specifying a fixed icon to use. There is no easy way to intercept this.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Microsoft Telnet crashes when run alongside Calmira
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Start Menu colour cannot be changed
\par 
\par Progress box icon cannot be changed.
\par 
\par The Alt key activates Calmira windows and flashes the title bar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you press the Alt key from one of Calmira's windows, all of the windows are brought to the front.  Also, the title bar of the active window flashes briefly when Alt is released.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The cursor is not released after right clicking during certain drags
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You should not right click while dragging files from the {\uldb Find Files dialog}{\v FindFiles} or {\uldb Bin}{\v Bin}
.  These windows use list boxes, and Calmira is unable to detect that files are being dragged.  The problem does {\i not} affect icon windows, where right clicking during a drag operation is essential for copying/moving files.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Association tab page displays blank registry entries
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Some registry entries which are incorrect can cause the combo box on the Association page of the {\uldb File Properties dialog}{\v FileProp}
 to display just "(No command specified)" lines with no file type description.  Do not associate files with these entries. 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Limitation on reported disk and file sizes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The largest whole number that Calmira can store is  2,147,483,647 which is equivalent to 2GB, so any disks or file systems that are larger than this will not have their sizes correctly reported.  Usually, sizes larger than 2GB will simply be reported as 2
GB.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Menu separators can become parent items
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You should not put a minus sign in front of parent menu captions, because they will be drawn as separator lines.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Menu separators can be "selected" using cursor keys
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The separators of a 3D Start Menu are implemented as real menu items because normal separators cannot have a 3D effect.  The highlight bar will disappear when it moves over such items.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adobe windows can't be closed with right click
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You may receive a general protection fault if you attempt to close a window by right clicking on the minimize or maximize box of Abobe programs such as Acrobat Reader and Photoshop.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Folder names must not contain trailing spaces
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you use an "open folder" style command from anywhere within Calmira, make sure that there are no trailing spaces in the folder name.  For example "c:\\games" is OK, but "c:\\
games " may cause problems because MS-DOS can find the directory, but the contents will not be accessible when Calmira appends a filename to the latter path.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Shortcuts accidentally created when opening from the Computer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When you double click on an icon in the Computer, you should not move the cursor out of the window until the program has finished loading.  If you move the cursor over the desktop during the load, a shortcut may be created because the program start-up has
 interrupted mouse messages, and Calmira believes that you are still dragging.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Minimized icon windows still show a pressed taskbar button
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Some dialog boxes from other programs are truncated by the Taskbar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This problem is being investigated.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Ampersand in menu captions
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 
To show "&" in a Start Menu caption, enter "&&" instead, otherwise the space after the "&" is treated as a keyboard mnemonic.  For the same reason, there is a problem with Calmira's message boxes, which display filenames containing the & character incorre
}{\f2 ctly as an underscore.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Progress bar colour must be silver
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The progress bar background should be always be set to "silver" or "btnface", even though there is an option to change it.  This is because the new Win95-style gauge does not paint its background colour properly.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Trouble}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 General Troubleshooting}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} troubleshooting; bugs; reporting bugs; help}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +
} 00000}} General Troubleshooting
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par If you are experiencing any difficulties that are not covered above, you can follow these guidelines to try and isolate the cause.  You should exit to DOS and run Windows again after trying out a software modification.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Visit the web site for the latest information
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you have Internet access, you can view up-to-date information about any bugs or incompatibilities that are discovered.  Otherwise, you can receive all published documentation if you order a new version by post.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Read the relevant help topics again
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Make sure that you are using Calmira correctly, and understand how to use the features.  Many "problems" are simply a misunderstanding of the way things work, or minor omissions such as forgetting to type the full pathname or the working directory. Consul
t your Windows and MS-DOS manuals where appropriate and check the Calmira Readme file too.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Find out how to repeat the problem
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You need to be able to write down a list of steps that can reliably reproduce the problems. An investigation often produces useful information and patterns, and you certainly need it if you want to report a bug.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Try to reproduce the problem on another computer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This is usually a good way to determine whether the problem lies with Calmira.  If it runs fine on a friend's PC, then find out what the differences are, and adjust your software configuration to match the other machine.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 If the cause is an unusual program
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Re
member that Calmira should not be expected to work with every program on the Internet .  If you're having problems with utilities that perform special operations or tricks with graphics or the user interface, then the best solution is not to use the confl
icting programs at the same time. 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Make sure there is enough memory and resources
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira's behaviour is undefined if memory or resources are very low.  You may eventually get error messages, but before that happens, there may be strange effects on the 
screen such as areas not being fully repainted.  You can try and make more resources and memory available by following the guidelines in the {\uldb Optimising Calmira}{\v OptimisingCalmira} and {\uldb Optimising Windows}{\v OptimisingWindows} topics.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Run Calmira as a normal Windows program
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If this improves the situation, then follow the instructions in Common Problems for resolving shell issues.  Calmira should behave almost identically when it is the shell, so if there {\i is}
 a difference, it could be due to other drivers or utilities being loaded in a different order.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Dealing with general protection faults
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When you get a GPF message, note down the filename in which the fault occurred (which could be a DLL file).  If it's not Calmira, find out which program uses the file and stop running it or disable the specific feature that uses the file.  Some of Calmira
's non-standard operations, such as right clicking to close windows, do not work with all programs.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Unload all other applications except Program Manager
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Of course, this is not applicable if your problem is to do with another application program, but if it appears to be confined to Calmira alone, then you should not run anything else while testing.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Unload all third party utilities 
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
This includes everything in your Program Manager Startup group and in Calmira's Startup menu.  Look in Win.ini for the Load= and Run= lines and comment them out if they are starting any programs. Also uninstall Type Manager and similar low-level utilities
.
\par 
\par Conflicts are most likely to occur when other utilities perform similar function to Calmira such as installing hooks to detect the mouse and keyboard, and modifying the behaviour of the desktop.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Run automatically loaded utilities later on
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you have programs that are causing problems in the Load= and Run= lines in Win.ini, or items in the Startup menu, try starting them manually after Calmira has completely finished loading and is idle.  If this works, you could write a script file to lau
nch them manually in future.
\par  
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Change your video driver to a simpler one
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you are using a third-party Super VGA driver, change to a generic Super VGA driver supplied by Microsoft, or the standard 640x480 VGA driver that is supplied with Windows.  Do not use more than 256 colours when testing for problems.
\par 
\par If your graphics card comes with extra utilities, use them to turn off any special features that your graphics hardware might have, and then remove the utilities from memory (unless Windows does not work without them).
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Uninstall potentially unstable drivers
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Look in the Drivers dialog of Control Panel.  If you've experienced any problems with some of the drivers before, then remove them.  Also unload plugins and other web browser related utilities, which are extremely prone to crashing.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Uninstall as many drivers and add-ons as possible
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Sound and music drivers are unlikely to cause problems, but graphics, screen, and font related drivers often interfere, so they should be removed if you can re-install them easily.  Also discon
nect network devices, and run Calmira from a local machine's standard hard disk with the minimum Windows setup.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Install a fresh copy of Windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you encounter a serious incompatibility problem but you really can't determine the driver or configuration that is causing the trouble, install another copy of Windows in a different directory.  Calmira should run correctly on all fresh installations, 
unless Windows is not configured properly for your hardware.  Once you get it working, install additional drivers and utilities one by one.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Reporting bugs}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 If you think you have really found a bug, I need to know as much as possible about the scenario in which it occurred.  This may include:}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  The exact actions you need to perform to }{\i\f2 repeat}{\f2  the problem}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Similar situations in which there are no problems (for comparison)}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  The software that need to be running for the error to occur}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Whether you had plenty of free memory}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  How often it occurs}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Whether it only occurs with certain Taskbar settings}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Whether it seems recoverable or crashes Windows}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Any trends or patterns you've noticed}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 \{}{\f2 bmc b.bmp}{\f2 \}}{\f2  Ideas you might have as to what is happening -- have a good think!}{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par You should also try and reproduce the problem with one of the standard Windows applets -- if you report a bug which only occurs with a program that I haven't got, then there is not much that can be done about it!  If the program is a small freeware, send 
the URL for downloading.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} KeyboardShortcuts}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Defining keyboard shortcuts}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} keyboard; shortcuts; hot-keys; start menu; windows; captions; [Keyboard]; Ctrl; Alt; Shift; activating}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Defining keyboard shortcuts
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 
Calmira lets you assign hot-keys to activate any window, menu item or desktop shortcut.  Since many programs define their own keyboard commands, Calmira only allows you to specify key combinations that include two or more out of Ctrl, Alt and Shift.}{\f2 

\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 Fill the [Keyboard] section of }{\f2 the INI file with keyboard mappings in the format caption=key.  For example,}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [Keyboard]
\par System=Shift+Ctrl+F1
\par Drive (C:)=Ctrl+Alt+C
\par c:\\windows=Ctrl+Alt+W
\par Notepad=Alt+Shift+N
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 
\par }{\f2 When you press keys together with Ctrl, Alt and Shift, Calmira searches the list and obtains the caption.  It then searches all Calmira windows, }{\f2\uldb shortcuts}{\v\f2 References}{\f2 , and the }{\f2\uldb Start Menu}{\v\f2 StartMenu}{\f2 
 for a match (in that order), and activates the object if a matching caption is found.  If you specify a folder which is not shown in a window, then the folder is opened for you.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 Finally, if nothing matches the text, the search extends to all windows on the screen, even those belonging to other applications.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 In the above example, Ctrl+Shift+F1 will restore the computer window.  Ctrl+Alt+C might activate a shortcut to drive C.  Ctrl+Alt+W will open the windows folder, and Shiftl+Alt+N could run Notepad
 if it's in the Start Menu.  
\par 
\par {\b Note}: to enable keyboard shortcuts to work from inside {\i any} Windows program, you must turn this feature on from the {\uldb System Properties Dialog}{\v SystemProp}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Keyboard activation of the Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
If you have a normal 102-key keyboard, you can use the above method and the caption of "Start" to assign a hot-key for the Start Menu, such as Ctrl+Alt+S.  If you have a Windows 95 keyboard, turn on the new key support from the Start Menu Properties dialo
g.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} CustomIcons}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
