\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} CustomBitmaps}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Customising your bitmaps}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} bitmaps; custom bitmaps; graphics; icons; start button; startbtn.bmp; explorer; explrico.bmp; start menu; startdef.bmp; banner}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Customising your bitmaps
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Some objects in Calmira use bitmap pictures instead of icons.  These are usually loaded from Calmira's program file, but you can override some of them by storing bitmaps with special filenames in Calmira's home directory.  Use Paintbrush to create standar
d 16 colour BMP files of the correct size.
\par 
\par The built-in bitmaps are available in an un-compiled form for you to modify -- look in the archive Help.zip (used to make this help file!), which is contained in Source.zip.  
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Start button}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc startbtn.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 26 x 18 pixels
\par {\b Filename}: Startbtn.bmp
\par {\b Comments}
\par The bottom left pixel is used as the transparent colour.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Explorer tree icons}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc explrico.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 192 x 16 pixels
\par {\b Filename}: Explrico.bmp
\par {\b Comments}
\par The bitmap is divided into 12 squares of 16 x 16 pixels.  The second and third squares are used to denote unknown and invalid drives, but they should never need to be used.  The last four squares are not fully used -- only the portions containing the defa
ult pictures are drawn to the screen.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Start Menu default icons}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc startdef.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 128 x 16 pixels\line {\b Filename}: Startdef.bmp
\par {\b Comments}
\par These are used for the small Start Menu items.  The first square should be left blank, or contain an image to use 
when the icon is specified to be <none>.  To use the new bitmap, you need to force the Start Menu to rebuild fully by editing any menu item icon, and pressing OK.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Start Menu banner}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc banner.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 27 x 256 pixels
\par {\b Filename}: Banner.bmp\line {\b Comments}
\par The above sample has been rotated.  Calmira automatically draws the top (white) edge of the Start Menu, but you need to supply the left edge.  If your Start Menu is taller than the banner, then the top portion of the banner is copied as often as required.
  
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Taskbar explorer icon}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc taskexp.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 16 x 16 pixels
\par {\b Filename}: Taskexp.bmp
\par The bottom left pixel is used as the transparency colour.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Taskbar folder icon}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc taskfold.bmp\}
\par {\b Size}: 16 x 16 pixels
\par {\b Filename}: Taskfold.bmp
\par The bottom left pixel is used as the transparency colour.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Environment}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Environment strings}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 environment; variables; %currentfolder%; %droppedfiles%; %calmira%; %windir%; %comspec%; %filename%; [Environment]; icons; shell; MS-DOS; files; folders; working directory}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {
\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Environment strings
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par {\uldb Shortcuts and aliases}{\v References} and {\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu} items can use MS-DOS environment variables in their settings.  The {\i Target}, {\i Parameters}, {\i Command}, {\i Icon file} and {\i Working Folder}
 fields (where applicable) can contain environment variable names (surrounded by %) like lines in MS-DOS batch files.  When you activate an object, the variable values are substituted for the names.
\par 
\par {\b\cf9 Special environment strings}{\b\cf9 
\par }{\b 
\par }{\b %currentfolder%} points to the directory showing in the active {\uldb icon window}{\v IconWindows} (if none are active, the variable is empty).  This is particularly useful for the {\i Working Folder}
 field when you want to create or modify files in the folder being displayed.  For instance, if you use it for a Notepad shortcut, Notepad will always start up in the directory you are viewing, so you can open and save/create files without having to hunt 
through the tree.
\par 
\par {\b %currentdrive%} contains the drive letter of the active icon window, and can be used in a similar way to %currentfolder%.  Additionally, it is also available while the Disk Properties dialog is open, so your disk utility can be targeted 
a specific drive.
\par 
\par {\b %droppedfiles%} is only valid immediately after files have been dropped into a file shortcut or alias.  It contains the list of filenames, and you can place it in the {\i Parameters}
 field to insert the filenames between other parameters.  If %droppedfiles% is omitted, the files are just appended to the parameters list.
\par 
\par {\b %filename%} is only valid when used with the Inspect program and default viewer (set from the {\uldb File System Properties}{\v FileSystemProp} dialog), and with entries in the [File Vie
wers] section.  It contains the name of the focused file, and is used to specify where on the command line to insert the filename.  If you omit this variable, the filename is appended to the end of the command line.
\par 
\par {\b %calmira%} points to the program's home directory.
\par 
\par {\b %comspec%} is defined automatically by MS-DOS, and gives the filename of the command interpreter (i.e. the MS-DOS shell), which is usually command.com.
\par 
\par {\b %windir%} is defined automatically by Windows and points to the Windows directory.
\par 
\par {\b Note}: a pre-defined environment variable that specifies a directory will not contain a final backslash unless it is the root directory.  For example %windir% usually has the value "c:\\windows" with no trailing backslash.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 User defined variables}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The [Environment] section of the INI file is added to Calmira's own environment after loading, so it has the same effect as the MS-DOS {\i set}
 command.  As with MS-DOS, don't add the % characters when making settings -- they are only used when variables are referred to.  
\par 
\par Use this section to store your own identifiers, such as folder locations and device names.  When a folder or device changes, just edit the environment and all shortcuts, aliases and menu items will refer to the right place automatically.  Settings made in
 the [Environment] section only affect Calmira, and does not require extra environment memory to be allocated.
\par 
\par {\b Example}{\b 
\par }Suppose your CD-ROM drive is F: but it might change in the near future.  You can add the line cdrom=f: to the environment, and make your shortcuts and Start Menu items point to %cdrom%\\
filename.exe.  When you change the value of %cdrom%, all your links are instantly updated.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b Note}: the icon {\i index} is not affected by the environment, so you should use something like %filename%(1) when re-mapping the icon file.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} CustomCaptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 CustomCaptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} drives; disk drives; captions; [Drives]; [Window Captions]; icon windows; computer; explorer; options; settings}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Custom captions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Renaming your drives
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Calmira does not distinguish between different types of floppy or removable drives.  You can override the default names given to disk drives by adding suitable descriptions to the [Drives] section of the INI file.  Assign a description to each drive lette
r that you want to describe, for example:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [Drives]
\par A=3\'bd" Floppy
\par B=5\'bc" Floppy
\par E=Syquest
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Descriptive captions on icon windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\uldb Icon windows}{\v IconWindows} usually display just the 8 letter part of the directory name in the title bar. If you use icon wind
ows to keep groups of aliases, you may want to give some of them proper captions.  All you need to do is add entries to the [Window Captions] section, for example:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 [Window Captions]
\par c:\\windows=Microsoft Windows
\par c:\\winword\\docs=Word Documents
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Calmira needs to search through the list every time you open a new window, so use these settings sparingly.  Also, remember to remove the entries when you have deleted the affected directories.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using file descriptions for window captions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you run Calmira with 4DOS file description support, then the descriptions are automatically used for window captions.  Simply set the option to use descriptions for captions, from the File System Properties dialog.

\par 
