\par Characters enclosed in single or double quotes are displayed "as is".
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} MiscColourValues}} Colour values
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 A colour setting can use one of these values :}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 clAqua, clBlack, clBlue, clDkGray, clFuchsia, clGray, clGreen, clLime, clLtGray, clMaroon, clNavy, clOlive, clPurple, clRed, clSilver, clTeal, clWhite, and clYellow}{\f2 
\par }{\f2  }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 clActiveBorder, clActiveCaption, clAppWorkSpace, clBackground, clBtnFace, clBtnHighlight, clBtnShadow, clBtnText, clCaptionText, clGrayText, clHighlight, clHighlightText, clInactive}{\f2 
Border, clInactiveCaption, clInactiveCaptionText, clMenu, clMenuText, clScrollBar, clWindow, clWindowFrame, and clWindowText.}{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par You can also specify a 4-byte hexadecimal number instead, the low three bytes representing RGB color intensities for blue, green, and red, respectively. The value 00FF0000 represents full-intensity, pure blue, 0000FF00 is pure green, and 000000FF is pure 
red. 00000000 is black and 00FFFFFF is white.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Scripts}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Using script files}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 scripts files; MS-DOS programs; batch files; commands; [Scripts]; WinScriptExtension; run; DosScriptFilename; DosTimerInterval; start.bat; explore.bat; folder.bat; TempScript; environment}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Using script files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Calmira can read and execute commands from text files, allowing you to carry out multiple operations in one go, like very simple batch files.  Because the same file can be accessed from both Windows and an MS-DOS box running in Enhanced Mode, scripts also
 allow you to run Windows programs from the DOS prompt like Windows 95 and NT.  
\par 
\par For example, you could type "start notepad" in an MS-DOS box to open the Windows Notepad.  Or you can create a shortcut that runs three programs instead of just one.
\par 
\par This article describes how to use script files with Calmira.  To learn how to write the scripts, see the topic {\uldb Format of a script file}{\v ScriptFormat}.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 {\cf9 Getting started}{\cf9 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Open the {\uldb System Properties}{\v SystemProp} dialog.
\par 2.  Enable both MS-DOS and Windows scripts check boxes.
\par 3.  Open your Autoexec.bat in Notepad.
\par 4.  Add the command {\f5 set }{\f5\fs18 TEMPSCRIPT=c:}{\f5\fs18 \\}{\f5\fs18 calmira.run}.
\par 5.  Reboot the computer.
\par 6.  Try out the Start and Folder batch files from a DOS window.
\par 7.  Using a text editor, create the following script named test.run:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 start
\par \pard \s4 c:\\windows
\par notepad
\par start
\par c:\\windows
\par \pard \s4\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 write
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 8.  Double click on the icon of test.run -- two programs load at once.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Running scripts from Windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The [Scripts] section has a WinScriptExt
ension setting that determines the files which Calmira will attempt to read when you double click on them in an icon window.  The default value is ".run", so all files with an extension of .run are assumed to be text scripts.  The System Properties dialog
 has a checkbox to run this feature on and off.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Running scripts from MS-DOS
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira checks for the presence of a special script file every few seconds.  By default, it looks for the file c:\\calmira.run.  This filename can be changed using the DosScriptF
ilename setting in the [Scripts] section.  The DosTimerInterval specifies the number of milliseconds between each disk check.  If you are caching your hard disk, 2000 to 3000 milliseconds gives a good response time without any noticeable disk activity.

\par 
\par If the file exists, Calmira loads it into memory, deletes it immediately, and then processes the lines one by one, just as if you had double clicked on the file from an icon window.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Generating scripts from MS-DOS
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Ordinary MS-DOS batch files can easily 
be used to create the scripts from the commands that you type (which is why each parameter is stored on a separate line!).  A batch file simply echoes a combination of keywords and its own parameters, and redirects them to be appended to the script file.

\par 
\par There is a slight problem -- since 
MS-DOS programs are pre-emptively multi-tasked under Enhanced Mode Windows 3.1, Calmira could try and read the script before the batch file has finished generating the lines.  So instead of writing the script directly, batch files should send the lines to
 another file, and then rename that file to the required script at the very end.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Sample batch files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The four supplied files, Start.bat, Folder.bat, Explore.bat and Open.bat expect an MS-DOS environment variable called TempScript, which points to a tempo
rary filename in the same directory as the target script file (so that a simple rename will make the script "appear" for Calmira).  You can set TempScript to c:\\calmira.tmp in your Autoexec.bat if the default c:\\calmira.run is looked for.
\par 
\par Study the batch files to see how they work.  Each one resembles an MS-DOS command, complete with error handling and help.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 OPEN batch command
\par \pard\plain \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\f2 The newer Open.bat replaces both Start and Folder commands.  It requires the supplied DirExist.exe utility to detect the existence of a directory.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }{\f2 To use Open, copy the file and DirExist.exe into a directory on the MS-DOS path.  Type Open /? for details.  You can use it to open folders, programs or documents, and it will try to determine a sensible choice.}{\f2 
\par }{\f2 
\par }\pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 DirExist returns an errorlevel of 1 if the directory parameter exists.  All root directories are onsidered to exist, even if the drive is invalid.  The Turbo Pascal source code is included.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Notes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Only one script can be executing at any one time.  Scripts cannot run themselves, or other scripts.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} ScriptFormat}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Format of a script file}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} script files; folder; start; explore; macro; activate; hot-keys; explorer; icon windows; running programs}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Format of a script file
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par A script is just a text file containing one or more "paragraphs" separated by blank lines.  A paragraph contains a keyword on the first line, followed by one or more parameter lines.  For example:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 <Keyword1>
\par <Parameter1>
\par <Parameter2>
\par 
\par <Keyword2>
\par <Parameter1>
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Each keyword is explained below:
\par 
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx1272\cellx6642\pard \s5\intbl Keyword\cell {\b\f5 Folder}\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx1272\cellx6642\pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Purpose\cell 
Opens an icon window\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 1\cell The current directory\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 2\cell 
The name of the directory to open\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 \cell \cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Keyword\cell {\b\f5 Start}\cell 
\pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Purpose\cell Executes commands in the same way as the Run dialog\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 1\cell 
The current directory\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 2\cell A command to execute\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter ...\cell 
A command to execute\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 \cell \cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Keyword\cell {\b\f5 Explore}\cell \pard\plain \intbl 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Purpose\cell Opens the Explorer tree\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 1\cell The directory to open\cell \pard\plain \intbl 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 \cell \cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Keyword\cell {\b\f5 Macro}\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl 
\f2\fs20\lang2057 Purpose\cell Executes a built-in command (see Start Menu Properties)\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 1\cell The built-in command to execute (e.g. $System)\cell \pard\plain 
\intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 \cell \cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Keyword\cell {\b\f5 Activate}\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain 
\s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Purpose\cell Simulates a hot-key combination being pressed\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx1272\cellx6642\pard\plain \s5\intbl \f2\fs20\lang2057 Parameter 1\cell 
The caption of the object to activate\cell \pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} OptimisingCalmira}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Optimising Calmira}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K}
 optimising; memory; resources; speed; graphics; icons; windows; menu items; start menu; shortcuts; aliases; dialog boxes; [Icons]; descriptions; searching for files; trash; bin}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Optimising Calmira
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart Optimising}{\*\bkmkend Optimising}
\par Many of these issues are inter-related, so more conservative settings are likely to use less memory and resources, {\i and} speed up your system.  Experiment to find the most effective setup for your PC.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using less resources
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Resources are mainly consumed by windows (which include shortcuts, buttons, list boxes etc.), menu items, and graphics objects such as bitmaps.  It is difficult to avoid using a lot of resources if you want an advanced graphical front-end, but if you are 
short of resources, you can use slightly less by changing the way you run Calmira.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\fi-270\li270\tx270 \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Use shortcuts sparingly -- each one requires one window for the icon and another one for the caption.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Make use of the multi-window closing facilities (keyboard commands and the desktop menu) to close unwanted icon windows as soon as you have finished using them.  As well as window handles, each icon window owns a set of popup menus too.

\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Close modeless dialog boxes (Find, Run etc.) as soon as possible.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Don't put too many items in the Start Menu, because each menu item uses up resources, and it's easy to forget how large the menu has become.
\par \pard \s5 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using less memory
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 If you only have 4MB RAM, then you should also read the topic on optimising Windows to make sure that memory is not being wasted.  
\par 
\par \pard \s5\fi-270\li270\tx270 \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Use aliases sparingly -- each alias can use over 3K when it is being displayed in a window.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Make use of the window closing facilities to close unnecessary icon windows, especially those containing many aliases.  
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Calmira's memory heap may become fragmented after long periods of use, and some memory will not be released even when you close ico
n windows.  The best way to prevent this problem is to limit the number of icon windows that are ever open at the same time, if they contain a large number of files.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Don't specify too many document icons in the [Icons] section, because once loaded (when Calmira needs to display it), a document icon is not unloaded until Calmira finishes.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Reduce the file types which are searched for icons.  This is useful if you have directories full of files containing icons, ICO files in particular.  Just searching EXE files is usually enough.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\} \tab Don't put too many items in the Start Menu, because each menu item uses a few hundred bytes of memory, and each small icon requires a 16x16 bitmap, which also consumes memory.
\par \pard \s5 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Speeding up Calmira
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard \s5\fi-270\li270\tx270 \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Reduce the file types which are searched for icons to just EXE files.  DLL files rarely contain useful information in their icons.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Turn off 4DOS description support if you never use it.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab 
Limit the number of icons used for document files to prevent Calmira from extracting too many icons.  You can override the registry settings by using the [Icons] section to map different file extensions to the same icon, which loads it only once.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Turn off the miniature icon view to make scrolling faster -- shrinking icons is a very slow process.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Turn off the solid highlight bar on the Start Menu to significantly increase drawing speed.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Turn off the option scroll views while scroll bars are dragged (in the System Properties dialog).
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab When searching for files, only specify one wildcard and clear the list between searches.  This lets Calmira bypass the checks for duplicate entries and is much faster for long lists.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Don't keep too much trash because the bin checks through the files when Calmira loads, to read the size and date attributes.
\par \pard \s5 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Streamlining the INI file
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 There are two sections in Calmira.ini which should be well maintained to avoid slowing down the program.  You should quit Calmira and edit the INI file with a text editor for this.
\par 
\par If your icon windows have their positions saved then you need to clear out the [Window Positions] section to prevent it becoming too big.  Calmira doesn't update this section when folders have been moved or deleted, and it can grow very quickly.  Also, ma
ke sure that the [Window Captions] section does not contain unnecessary entries. 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} OptimisingWindows}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Optimising Windows}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} optimising; memory; resources; speed; system.ini; 32-bit file access; Buffers; LastDrive; shell; environment; programs;
 utilities; Smartdrv; EMM386; MemMaker; graphics; video drivers; wallpaper; fonts}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Optimising Windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This article contains some suggestions for getting the most out of your setup. MS-DOS and Windows 3.1 have been around for so long that many people have forgotten to check their configuration regularly.  Calmira is a demanding program, so if your computer
 is not very powerful, you should try and get the maximum performance from your operating system.
\par 
\par Always back up important data and back up system files before you modify them.  Please remember that if you are not confident enough to modify your configuration, then don't attempt it!  You must know {\i exactly}
 what you are doing to avoid damaging your system.  
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Windows for Workgroups 3.11
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Windows for Workgroups 3.11 supports for 32-bit file access which can speed up Calmira because it relies heavily on reading directories and files.  Calmira was mainly developed on Windows 3.1, but as of Version 2.0, W
indows for Workgroups 3.11 is the recommended operating system, together with MSDOS 6.22.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Streamline MS-DOS
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-270\li270\tx270 \f2\fs20\lang2057 \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab If you use SmartDrive, set Buffers=10, otherwise set Buffers=30.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Set the LastDrive parameter to the lowest letter possible.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Make sure your shell is not allocating too much environment space.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab 
Check all your device drivers and TSR programs again.  Do you really need them, and are they configured optimally?  Read through their manuals and see if there are any extra switches you can use to improve performance.  Throw out unnecessary DOS utilities
 like Setver.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab If you run memory-resident programs solely for use with Windows, you can move them into WinStart.bat in your Windows directory, which is executed only after Windows has started 386 Enhanced Mode.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Use SmartDrive to cache only floppy and CD-ROM drives if you are using the Windows for Workgroups disk cache.  You can reduce the SmartDrive buffer to 128K or less while running Windows.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Turn off expanded memory support using MemMaker if your DOS programs don't need EMS. Otherwise, add Min=0 on the EMM386 command line.
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard \s5\fi-270\li270\tx270 \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Run MemMaker every time you modify your DOS settings.  When asked, choose to make a custom setup, scan upper memory aggressively, optimise for use with Windows, make use of the m
onochrome memory region (see later) and discard existing EMM386 settings.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Follow the instructions in your MS-DOS manual for using MemMaker to optimise multiple configurations and find the best loading order for drivers.
\par 
\par \{bmc b.bmp\}\tab Remember that config.sys has an Install= setting for loading certain memory resident programs which are not already loaded into upper memory blocks.  See your MSDOS manual for more details.
\par \pard \s5 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Improving Windows performance
\par {\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
\par }\pard \s3\fi-270\li270\sa30\tx270 {\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \{}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 bmc b.bmp}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \}}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \tab }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
Use a suitable video driver.  Using a display driver with a high resolution or large number of colours results in slower display performance.  Calmira is graphics intensive, and you are recommended to use a 16 colour VGA driver or 256 colour generic Super
}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057  VGA driver from Microsoft. }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
\par }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
\par }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \{}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 bmc b.bmp}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \}}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 \tab }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
Look for the latest video and sound driver upgrades from the original hardware manufacturers, or from Microsoft.}{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
\par }{\plain \f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 
