\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Compiling the help file
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The RTF file was created using Microsoft Word for Windows 2.0, but should be editable in other word processors that support RTF.  
\par 
\par You will need Microsoft's protected mode help compiler (HCP.EXE) to rebuild the help file because there seems to be too much data for HC31 to handle.  Download HCP from Microsoft's software library at:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 ftp://ftp.microsoft.com/Softlib/MSLFILES/HC505.EXE 
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par The program is DPMI compliant, so you can set up a PIF file to give it 384K of conventional memory and no EMS or XMS; 
it runs very smoothly in the background. The supplied Make.bat copies the source text to a separate file, which avoids file sharing problems if you have the RTF file open in a word processor.  
\par 
\par The compression option has been deliberately turned off, because the resulting compressed help file actually results in a bigger Zip file -- presumably, PKZIP does a much better job when the help compiler doesn't get involved!
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Technical}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Technical Notes}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} technical notes}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Technical Notes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 No stack checking!
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Stack checking has been turned off to marginally increase performance, and the stack size increased to 20KB for the main program and decreased to 8KB for the Taskbar.  Calmira actually needs a sizeable stack to handle recursion simultaneously with backgro
und "threads" such as file copying and re-building of the Start Menu.   
\par 
\par While it is not totally safe to remove the stack checking code, the risk of overflowing the stack is small.  All recursive procedures and functions used in t
he main program have activation records much less than 256 bytes, and they are mostly involved with travelling the directory tree, which must be limited to 79 character pathnames anyway.  Even with four simultaneous threads at their deepest point of recur
sion, there should be more than enough stack for the VCL and other transient function calls.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Use of undocumented functions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira uses two features of Windows that are not documented in the API help file, although they are generally known.  The first
 is the drag-drop server implementation in DropServ.pas, which builds the memory object that accompanies the WMDropFiles message.  The second is the WMSysCommand parameter codes used in CalForm.pas for window resizing.  File System Change Notification, as
 described by Schulman et al, has not been used in this release.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Exported interface
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Calmira 2.2 does not have an API, DDE or otherwise, for communication with other programs.    See the CalMsgs.pas unit for details of internal messages that could be utilised.
\par 
\par There is one reserved and currently unused command for the WMCalmira registered message, which is CMReloadOptions.  This causes as many settings as possible to be reloaded from the INI file, and might be used by a future configuration utility.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Tip of the Day file format
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Tips are stored in a plain text file, which has a number on the first line indicating the number of entries in the file.  Blank lines are ignored, and lines beginning with semi-colons are considered comments.  All other lin
es contain tips, and should be less than 255 characters in length.
\par 
\par To count the number of tips in a modified tips file, use the CountTip utility supplied in one of the source archives as both a DOS executable and the Turbo Pascal code.  Run it using:
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 counttip < tips.txt
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This will check each tip for the length limitation, as well as output the number of tips found.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Possible causes of incompatibility
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The only "unusual" code that Calmira contains resides in the DLL file, which implements hooks for the mouse, shell, window procedures and keyboard. Their potential impact on stability has not been investigated. The desktop window is subclassed to capture 
right clicks, but only for convenience -- it is also possible to move the code into the mouse hook to avoid the problems caused by multiple utilities subclassing the desktop.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Alias file format
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Each alias is written to disk as a data stream consisting 
of a signature, the reference information, and an icon.  Strings are stored as variable length fields: one length byte, followed by the string data bytes.  Integers are 16-bit and booleans occupy one byte.
\par 
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx2007\cellx2982\cellx6627\pard \s5\intbl {\b\ul Field}{\b\ul \cell }{\b\ul Type}{\b\ul \cell }{\b\ul Comments}{\b\ul \cell }\pard\plain \intbl \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\b\f2\ul \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx2007
