\par TIcon.SaveToStream is used to write the icon data.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Bugs in previous source files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
A large number of corrections have been made to all files from Calmira 1.0, and the What's New topic only lists some of the more obvious flaws.  If you are using any of the code in other projects, you should check with the files in this package.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Translating}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Translating into another language}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Translating into another language
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Editing the forms
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The text embedded into the form files can be easily found by opening the DFM files in text mode from the Delphi IDE (use the Open File command).  You are less likely to miss any strings compared to editing the captions using the Object Inspector.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Editing embedded code strings
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Most of the language specific strings used within the code have been gathered into two files. The first is a unit called Embed.pas, which contains some string constants that need to be stored in typed constants.  Each string constant has a prefix of SS.

\par 
\par There are a few additional strings inside some of the component units, such as the version information component, which can also be modified if required.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Plural words
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The Strings unit declares the OneItem array which is used to add an "s" character to make some nouns plural where necessary.  You should use the Object Browser to search for all references to this array, and modify the code to use the grammar of the targe
t language.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Editing the string tables
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The second localis
ation unit is called Locale.pas and contains a list of integer constants (prefixed by S in standard Delphi fashion) which are used to give indices to the string tables.  You can leave this file alone and go straight to Locale.rc which is processed by the 
resource compiler.  After translating the strings, build Locale.res by running
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s4 \f5\fs18\cf1\lang2057 brc -r -folocale.res locale.rc
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Distributing}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Distributing modified versions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Distributing modified versions
